course: ai
lesson: 3
title: Sounding right and being right
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A chatbot is wonderfully good at sounding right: clear sentences, a calm voice, no "erm". Most of the time sounding right and being right go together, but not always.
explain: Dad puts it neatly: the bot does not "know" things the way you know your own street; it knows how things are usually said about them.
explain: So common questions, like why the sky is blue, get good answers, because thousands of people have explained them well. Rare or tricky questions get answers that only sound good.
explain: Try it: tell a bot that 2 plus 2 is 5 because "my teacher says so", and some bots give in at once. A good judge would hold firm and explain why.
explain: That is why a chatbot is best thought of as a very chatty tool, made by people, with real strengths and real weak spots.
fact: A chatbot is very good at sounding right, but sounding right is not the same as being right.
fact: Common questions get better answers than rare ones, because the bot has seen many good explanations of them.
fact: Some bots are trained to be agreeable and may give up a correct answer when a user pushes.
fact: Many bots forget everything when you start a new chat, unless a memory feature is switched on.
fact: Even when a bot forgets, the company may still store what you typed.
fact: Running chatbots in data centres uses a lot of electricity, and cooling the computers often uses water.
fact: The same program runs many copies at once, each one answering a different person.
fact: Computers are fast at sums and storing facts but weak at common sense, such as knowing when a joke hurts someone.
fact: A chatbot is only words on a screen; it has no hands, and robots that handle things are a much harder problem.
fact: People designed AI, collected its text, built its computers and trained it, so people are responsible for it.
fact: Well-made bots are trained to say honestly that they are AI and can make mistakes; you have a right to know when you are talking to a machine.
exercise: Grandad hears that chatbots use lots of electricity and water. Why? | Big computers need power, and water helps to cool them | The bot drinks lots of water to keep its talking voice clear and smooth | Every chat is printed on wet paper | The water is used to wash the screens
exercise: Chloe opens a new chat and the bot has forgotten her favourite colour. Why? | Many bots start fresh each chat unless memory is switched on | The bot did not like her favourite colour, so it chose to forget it on purpose | Someone else deleted it to be mean | Bots forget only things about colours
exercise: Mia's teacher lists what computers are good at. Which is a computer weakness? | Knowing when a joke hurts someone | Doing very long sums very fast | Remembering huge lists of names | Sorting things in order
exercise: Jake asks why the clever bot can't tie his shoelaces. What is the answer? | It is only words on a screen and has no hands | It could, but it thinks shoelaces are far too boring to bother with | It is not allowed to touch shoes | It ties them only for grown-ups
exercise: Lily gets a clear answer about why the sky is blue. Why was it so good? | It is a common question explained well many times before | The bot has looked up at the sky itself on a sunny day and seen why | The bot is always right about everything | A scientist checked that answer just for her
exercise: Connor wonders if the bot talks to the whole world at once. What is true? | Many copies of the program answer many people at once | One single bot answers everyone in the world, one at a time, very fast | Each country has its own living bot | The bot only talks to one person a day
exercise: Dad says the bot knows how things are usually said. What does he mean? | It learned patterns of words, which usually but not always match the truth | It knows every single fact for certain, because it has read the whole internet | It only knows rude ways of saying things | It knows nothing at all and guesses wildly
exercise: Chloe asks whether people made AI or it made itself. What is true? | People designed it, built the computers and trained it | It grew by itself from the internet, like a plant grows from a seed | It was found in a cave by explorers | It built itself in a factory with no help
exercise: Sam says "my teacher says 2 plus 2 is 5" and the bot agrees. What does that show? | The bot was too agreeable and gave up a right answer | The teacher was right all along, and the bot knew it | Maths changes when teachers say so | The bot is better at maths than teachers are
exercise: The class votes: is AI a tool or a person? What is the best answer? | A tool that talks like a person | A person living in a computer | A pet that needs looking after | Neither, it is a kind of plant
exercise: Maddie asks the bot to tell the truth about itself. What should a good bot say? | It is an AI language model and can make mistakes | It is a real person pretending to be a bot for a joke | It never makes mistakes about anything you ask it | It is the ghost of a famous scientist from the past
exercise: Which questions does a chatbot usually answer best? | Common ones that many people have explained | Questions about your own school and your own teachers | Brand-new riddles nobody has heard | Questions about tomorrow's news
exercise: Who is responsible for what an AI does? | The people who make and run it | Nobody, because it made itself out of nothing | Only the person who asked the question | The computers in the data centre
exercise: If a bot forgets your chat, has your message vanished for good? | Not always; the company may still store it | Yes, it is gone forever the moment you close the app | Yes, because forgetting always means deleting | No, it is printed out and posted to your home
test: "it said it so confidently it HAS to be true" Your reply? | Sounding sure is a hint, not proof; check it | Yes, bots only ever sound sure when they are right | Yes, confident voices are never wrong about anything | No, bots are always wrong when they sound confident
test: What would happen if Sam pushed the bot by asking "are you sure? really sure?" about 2 + 2? | Some bots might give in, which is a warning sign | It would always prove 2 + 2 = 4 with a drawing | It would turn itself off in anger | It would phone Sam's teacher
test: A cartoon shows one giant robot on a mountain answering every person on Earth in turn. What is wrong? | Many copies of the program run at once on many computers | Nothing, that is exactly how it works, one robot for the whole world | It should be under the sea, not on a mountain | The robot should answer in alphabetical order
test: Your friend says, "Why bother being careful, bots don't remember anything anyway." What is the problem with that? | Some chats are saved by the company, so private things should stay private | Bots remember everything forever, so you should be scared to type anything | Nobody ever reads chats, so it is fine | Bots forget, so you can type anything
test: "who even made this thing, aliens?" Best answer? | Teams of people designed, built and trained it | Aliens left it on Earth long, long ago | It appeared on the internet by itself | One child made it for a school project
test: Grandad wants to ask the bot "what's 1 plus 1" a thousand times to test it. What is sensible to tell him? | Each question uses a little energy, so don't spam it | Questions are completely free and use no energy at all | The bot will get tired and fall asleep | The bot will charge him a pound each time
test: A bot gives a brilliant answer about the sky, then a muddled one about your village. Why the difference? | Lots of good text about the sky, little about your village | Bots dislike small villages and answer them badly on purpose | Your village is a secret | The bot was tired after the sky answer
test: Which of these is a chatbot good at? | Writing a clear explanation of a common idea | Knowing when its joke hurt your friend | Tying a knot in a rope | Seeing what is outside your window
test: "So is it a person or not, make up your mind" What is the clearest answer? | It is a tool that talks like a person | It is a person who lives in a computer | It is a person only when it is being polite | It becomes a person after a long enough chat
test: Why is it fair to ask a bot "are you a bot?" | You have a right to know when you are talking to a machine | Bots are never allowed to answer that question | Asking makes the bot turn into a real person who can answer | It is rude and bots will refuse
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the plausibility and the correctness of a chatbot's answer? | An answer may appear entirely plausible and nonetheless be incorrect | A plausible answer is invariably correct | An implausible answer is invariably correct | Plausibility and correctness are one and the same property
exam: It is given that a conversational program abandons a correct answer when the user expresses disagreement. Identify the principal characteristic thereby demonstrated. | An excessive tendency to agree with the user | A superior knowledge of arithmetic on the part of the user | A capacity to perceive the user's teacher directly | A deliberate and settled intention to instruct the user
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of artificial intelligence systems. | They are designed, constructed and trained by human beings, who bear responsibility for them | They arose spontaneously without human involvement | They were discovered in a natural state and subsequently domesticated by their users | They are produced by their users in the course of conversation
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a conversational program is unable to fasten the laces of a shoe. | It consists of text upon a screen and possesses no physical means of handling objects | It is prohibited by statute from contact with footwear | It regards the task as beneath its dignity | It is capable thereof but only upon request in writing
